1)You've deployed a critical microservice application on OpenShift, and users are reporting intermittent 503 Service Unavailable errors. How would you approach troubleshooting this?
I would approach the 503 issue systematically: first verify pod health and probe configurations, ensure the service endpoints are correctly mapped, 
check the OpenShift router and route configurations, and then inspect resource usage and application logs.
I would also validate recent deployments, scaling behavior,and any network/service-mesh issues. 
This layered approach helps identify whether the 503 originates from the application layer, service layer, or ingress/router layer

2): A developer accidentally pushed a large, unoptimized Docker image (2GB+) to your internal OpenShift image registry.
How would you handle this situation and prevent it from happening again?
I would first clean up the large image and run image pruning to reclaim registry space. 
Then I’d work with the developer to rebuild the image using multi-stage builds, minimal base images, and .dockerignore. To prevent recurrence,
I’d enforce quotas on image sizes, add CI/CD checks to block large images before pushing, schedule registry image pruning, and educate developers on container best practices. 
This ensures our registry remains optimized, cost-efficient, and compliant with our standards.”

